{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 2 'T-6|capitalize_words' tasks.md && git show --stat --oneline b147246 && git show --format=fuller --no-ext-diff -- tasks.md b147246 && sed -n '1,220p' docs/plans/2026-08-28-1433-feat-capitalize-words-plan.md && sed -n '1,220p' toolkit/text.py && git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"9-- [x] T-4 `slugify(text)` in `toolkit/text.py` lowercases the text and then replaces every run of characters outside `[a-z0-9]` with a hyphen, so any non-ASCII letter is destroyed rather than carried: an accented vowel, a ligature, or a non-Latin script collapses to a hyphen and a title made mostly of them slugifies to the empty string. Make `slugify` transliterate Latin letters carrying diacritics to their unaccented ASCII form before the character class runs, so an accented vowel becomes its plain vowel rather than a hyphen, while a character with no ASCII equivalent still becomes a separator as it does today. Keep the existing behavior exactly for input that is already pure ASCII, including the leading and trailing hyphen strip and the collapsing of a run of separators to one. Add tests in `tests/test_text.py` covering an accented vowel mid word, a word that is entirely accented, a character with no ASCII equivalent, and one existing pure ASCII case re-asserted so a regression there is caught. The full suite must stay green. (da549141ae34f604166b580ba009718945a15c65)\n10-\n11:- [x] T-6 Add `capitalize_words(text)` to `toolkit/text.py`. (1ce15ab)\nb147246 chore: mark T-6 landed in tasks.md\n tasks.md | 2 ++\n 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)\ncommit b14724659b6e22e81f0ecf5cf5d48a69e6999b29\nAuthor:     Phillip Gutowski <relay@example.com>\nAuthorDate: Fri Aug 28 14:43:57 2026 -0400\nCommit:     Phillip Gutowski <relay@example.com>\nCommitDate: Fri Aug 28 14:43:57 2026 -0400\n\n    chore: mark T-6 landed in tasks.md\n\ndiff --git a/tasks.md b/tasks.md\nindex 3a7a52b..32fb513 100644\n--- a/tasks.md\n+++ b/tasks.md\n@@ -7,3 +7,5 @@\n - [x] T-3 Add `clamp(value, low, high)` to `toolkit/stats.py`. Return `low` when the value is below it, `high` when above, otherwise the value itself. Raise `ValueError` when `low` is greater than `high`. Add tests in `tests/test_stats.py` covering below, above, inside, at each bound, and the inverted bounds error. (bc97883d855073553131ffba49d6969aa92f408f)\n \n - [x] T-4 `slugify(text)` in `toolkit/text.py` lowercases the text and then replaces every run of characters outside `[a-z0-9]` with a hyphen, so any non-ASCII letter is destroyed rather than carried: an accented vowel, a ligature, or a non-Latin script collapses to a hyphen and a title made mostly of them slugifies to the empty string. Make `slugify` transliterate Latin letters carrying diacritics to their unaccented ASCII form before the character class runs, so an accented vowel becomes its plain vowel rather than a hyphen, while a character with no ASCII equivalent still becomes a separator as it does today. Keep the existing behavior exactly for input that is already pure ASCII, including the leading and trailing hyphen strip and the collapsing of a run of separators to one. Add tests in `tests/test_text.py` covering an accented vowel mid word, a word that is entirely accented, a character with no ASCII equivalent, and one existing pure ASCII case re-asserted so a regression there is caught. The full suite must stay green. (da549141ae34f604166b580ba009718945a15c65)\n+\n+- [x] T-6 Add `capitalize_words(text)` to `toolkit/text.py`. (1ce15ab)\n---\ntitle: Capitalize Words Helper - Plan\ntype: feat\ndate: 2026-08-28\nartifact_contract: ce-unified-plan/v1\nartifact_readiness: implementation-ready\nproduct_contract_source: ce-plan-bootstrap\nexecution: code\n---\n\n## Goal Capsule\n\n- **Objective:** Callers can normalize a phrase into title-style words with predictable casing and spacing.\n- **Means:** Add a small whitespace-tokenizing helper beside the existing text helpers (KTD1).\n- **Authority hierarchy:** This plan defines the requested behavior. Existing helpers and unittest coverage define local style.\n- **Stop conditions:** The helper matches all requested casing examples, collapses whitespace when joining words, and the unittest suite passes.\n- **Execution profile:** Single-session, single-unit implementation.\n- **Tail ownership:** The implementing session commits the helper, its tests, and this plan on `relay/T-6`.\n\n## Product Contract\n\n### Summary\n\nAdd `capitalize_words(text)` as a public helper in `toolkit/text.py`.\nIt converts each whitespace-delimited word to an uppercase first character followed by lowercase remaining characters, then joins words with one space.\n\n### Problem Frame\n\nThe toolkit has small, reusable text transformations but no helper for consistently formatted words.\nCallers would otherwise have to repeat casing and whitespace normalization logic.\n\n### Requirements\n\n- R1. `capitalize_words(text)` splits the input on whitespace and joins the resulting words with a single space.\n- R2. Each output word has its first character capitalized and every remaining character lowercased.\n- R3. Coverage proves the helper for a lowercase phrase, an already-capitalized phrase, and an all-caps phrase.\n\n### Scope Boundaries\n\n- The helper does not preserve original whitespace runs.\n- The helper does not add a broader text-formatting API or modify existing helpers.\n\n---\n\n## Planning Contract\n\n### Key Technical Decisions\n\n- KTD1. **Use the language's word-casing operation after whitespace splitting.** This directly expresses R1 and R2 while matching the repository's preference for small standard-library helpers.\n\n### Assumptions\n\n- Python's default whitespace splitting is the intended interpretation of \"split on whitespace.\"\n\n---\n\n## Implementation Units\n\n### U1. Add the word-capitalization helper and coverage\n\n- **Goal:** Provide `capitalize_words(text)` and prove its requested casing behavior.\n- **Requirements:** R1, R2, R3\n- **Dependencies:** None.\n- **Files:**\n  - `toolkit/text.py`\n  - `tests/test_text.py`\n- **Approach:** Add the helper alongside the existing public text functions. Split the input into words, apply the standard word-casing operation to each token, and join the results using one literal space.\n- **Patterns to follow:** Follow the existing concise function docstrings in `toolkit/text.py` and the one-assertion unittest methods in `tests/test_text.py`.\n- **Test scenarios:**\n  - A lowercase phrase such as `\"hello world\"` becomes `\"Hello World\"`.\n  - An already-capitalized phrase such as `\"Hello World\"` remains `\"Hello World\"`.\n  - An all-caps phrase such as `\"HELLO WORLD\"` becomes `\"Hello World\"`.\n- **Verification:** The full unittest suite passes with the new helper and the existing text helpers unchanged.\n\n---\n\n## Verification Contract\n\n| Command | Applies to | Done signal |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `python -m unittest discover` | U1 and repository gate | The complete unittest suite reports success. |\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- `toolkit/text.py` exports `capitalize_words(text)` with the R1 and R2 behavior.\n- `tests/test_text.py` contains the three requested phrase-casing scenarios.\n- `python -m unittest discover` passes.\n\"\"\"Text helpers.\"\"\"\n\nimport re\nimport string\nimport unicodedata\n\n\ndef truncate(text, limit):\n    \"\"\"Cut `text` to at most `limit` characters, ending in an ellipsis when it was cut.\"\"\"\n    if limit < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"limit must be at least 1\")\n    if len(text) <= limit:\n        return text\n    return text[: max(limit - 1, 0)] + \"…\"\n\n\ndef capitalize_words(text):\n    \"\"\"Capitalize each whitespace-delimited word in `text`.\"\"\"\n    return string.capwords(text)\n\n\ndef _transliterate_diacritics(text):\n    \"\"\"Replace a character with its ASCII base letter when its Unicode decomposition reduces to exactly one ASCII letter (covers accented Latin letters and similar compatibility forms), leaving any other character unchanged.\"\"\"\n    result = []\n    for char in unicodedata.normalize(\"NFC\", text):\n        if ord(char) < 128:\n            result.append(char)\n            continue\n        base = \"\".join(c for c in unicodedata.normalize(\"NFKD\", char) if not unicodedata.combining(c))\n        if len(base) == 1 and base.isascii() and base.isalpha():\n            result.append(base)\n        else:\n            result.append(char)\n    return \"\".join(result)\n\n\ndef slugify(text):\n    \"\"\"Lowercase `text` and replace runs of non-alphanumeric characters with a single hyphen.\"\"\"\n    return re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]+\", \"-\", _transliterate_diacritics(text).lower()).strip(\"-\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
